      <title>This Norwegian village is one of the fastest-warming places on Earth. How can its people save it?</title>
      <link>https://www.theguardian.com/environment/2026/oct/21/norway-svalbard-environment-longyearbyen-climate-heating-glacier-permafrost</link>
      <description>&lt;p&gt;As glaciers melt and permafrost thaws, Longyearbyen, on the remote Arctic islands of Svalbard, faces climate-induced problems from tap water to housebuilding to landslides&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;• &lt;a href="https://www.theguardian.com/environment/2026/oct/19/tap-water-no-longer-drinkable-norwegian-town-climate-related-thawing-heavy-metals-arctic-longyearbyen-svalbard"&gt;Heavy metals make Norwegian village’s tap water toxic&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;On a rugged mountain plateau of snow-dusted rocks, in the remote archipelago of Svalbard, a glaciologist, Andy Hodson, points out the “ears” of ice, white against the rock, that gave Foxfonna glacier its name, after the arctic fox.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;The small glacier, on a mountain 8 miles (15km) outside the Norwegian village of Longyearbyen, is a popular summer hike for tourists who come here seeking the Arctic experience.&lt;/p&gt; &lt;a href="https://www.theguardian.com/environment/2026/oct/21/norway-svalbard-environment-longyearbyen-climate-heating-glacier-permafrost"&gt;Continue reading...&lt;/a&gt;</description>
